File Organization
    .method of storing data records in a file so efficient access
    .determines how data is arranged, stored, and retrieved from physical storage
        .helps in faster selection of records
        .different operations like inserting and deleting different records are faster
        .prevents insertion of duplicate records via various operations
        .helps minimize data storage costs 



    .Types 
        .Sequential file Organization
            .files stored one after another in sequential manner
                
                .Pile-file method
                    .store files in the order in which they are inserted into tables

                .Sorted file method
                    .records are sorted in ascending or descending manner 
                    .the sorting may be based on Primary Key or any other key

                .Heap file Organization
                    .works with data blocks 
                    .records are inserted at the end of the file, into the data blocks
                    .no sorting or ordering is required 



                
File uploads
    .its the importing external files into a DB table 
    .can be storing the actual file as binary data directly inside the DB
     OR storing the file as an external server and saving only its path string in the DB






Rate Limiting in System Design
    .technique used to regulate how quickly a system processes or serves incoming requests or actions 
    .limits the quantity or frequency of client requests to prevent overload, maintain stability, and ensure fair 
      resource distribution 
    .reduces the risk of resource abuse and denial-of-service (DoS) attacks, improving performance, reliablity, and Security
    .used in web servers, APIs, network traffic mgt, and DB access
    .ensures fair resource usage among multiple users and improves overall system reliability and performance



    .Use Cases 
        .API rate limiting - to control the volume of client requests, ensure fair access to resources, and prevent abuse
        .Web server rate limiting - as a defense against denial of service attacks and to prevent server overload
        .database rate limitation - keeps the DB server from experiencing undue strain and preserve DB performance
        .login rate restriction - to stop password guessing and brute-force assaults 


    Types of R.L.
        .IP-based 
            .limits the number of requests a client can make based on their IP address within a specific time period
            .used to prevent abuse like bots and denial of service attacks
            .blocks excessive traffic from a single source
            .can be bypassed using VPNs, proxies, or botnets
            .may block legitimate users using the same IP address

        .Server-based
            .limits the number of requests a server can handle within a specific time period to prevent overload and maintain performance
            .ensures fair resource usage so no single user degrades performance
            .can be bypassed if requests are spread across multiple servers
            .legitimate users may face delays if limits are too strict or traffic is high 

        .Geography-based 
            .limits requests based on the geographic location of the user's IP address 
            .useful for controlling traffic from specific regions and improving security or compliance 
            .reduces malicious traffic from high-risk regions/ complying with regional laws and regulations
            .can be bypassed using VPNs or proxy servers/ may block genuine users traveling or using international networks 

        

    Rate Limiting Algorithms 
        .Token bucket 
            .controls request rate by adding tokens to a bucket at a fixed rate 
            .each incoming request consumes a token, and requests are only allowed if tokens are available 
        .Leaky bucket 
            .controls the rate of rewquests by processing them at a fixed and constant rate 
            .incoming requests are placed in a queue, and handled in order at a steady rate, regardless of how fast they arrive 
        .Fixed window counting
            .limits requests by counting how many requests are made within a fixed time window 
            .if requests exceed a defined limit, further requests are rejected until the next window starts 
        .Sliding window log
            .algorithm tracks each request by storing its timestamp and calculates the request rate over a moving time window 
            .older requests outside the window are removed, ensuring accurate rate limiting 



